13:10:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Vietnam is generally very welcoming and safe for travelers, but petty theft and scams can happen—especially in busy tourist areas, markets, nightlife districts, and on crowded transport. A few practical habits go a long way: - **Carry only what you need.** Leave your passport, spare bank cards, most cash, and valuables in a hotel safe or securely locked luggage. Carry a photocopy/digital copy of your passport separately. - **Use a secure day bag.** Choose a cross-body bag or small backpack with zippers that close fully. Keep it in front of you in crowds. Avoid leaving phones or wallets in back pockets or open tote bags. - **Watch for motorbike snatch theft.** In larger cities such as Ho Chi Minh City and Hanoi, thieves on motorbikes may grab phones or bags near the curb. Keep your phone close, avoid using it while standing at the road edge, and wear a cross-body strap away from traffic. Do not wrap bag straps around your arm or body tightly enough to be dragged if someone grabs it. - **Be discreet with valuables.** Avoid flashing expensive watches, jewelry, cameras, or large amounts of cash. Take out money privately rather than at the street. - **Use ATMs carefully.** Prefer ATMs inside banks, malls, or reputable hotels. Cover the keypad, inspect for anything unusual, and decline help from strangers. Keep an eye on your card. - **Use reputable transport.** Book through your hotel, established taxi companies, ride-hailing apps such as Grab, or reputable tour operators. Confirm the fare/meter where relevant and avoid unlicensed drivers aggressively soliciting rides. - **Keep bags attended.** Do not leave belongings on café chairs, in scooter baskets, or unattended on buses/trains. On overnight transport, keep essentials and valuables on your person or secured while sleeping. - **Be alert in crowded places.** Markets, walking streets, attractions, bus stations, and festivals are common places for pickpocketing. Keep zippers closed and avoid distractions from overly persistent strangers. - **Separate your money.** Keep a small amount of daily cash accessible and store backup cash/cards in a different secure location. - **Protect digital access.** Use a screen lock and tracking feature on your phone, back up photos, and consider an eSIM/physical SIM so you can contact banks or your accommodation if needed. Avoid sensitive banking over unsecured public Wi‑Fi. - **Get travel insurance.** Ensure it covers theft, medical needs, and electronics if those matter to you. Keep serial numbers and receipts for valuable equipment. If something is stolen, cancel cards promptly, use device-tracking/remote-lock features, notify your accommodation, and report it to local police if needed for an insurance claim. Stay aware rather than anxious—most visits are trouble-free.
